<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Council – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
        <td style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Share Our Voice in the Community</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify a speaker's point of view in a simple presentation or lecture. Interactive: Discuss information from audiovisual presentations like videos or movies.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the speaker's perspective and intentions in presentations on local issues, such as public work projects or environmental initiatives (e.g., "The council announced plans to improve the water system to address shortages. What is the intended impact of this change?"). Can interpret and discuss details from audiovisual presentations on community topics (e.g., "According to the speaker, I believe she means that by discussing recycling, we can help people change their habits, which is important."). Can ask and answer questions about main ideas in video presentations (e.g., "What did you think the main idea was?" "I think it was about the structure of town management.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by displaying images of local community facilities (e.g., a park, a school, a community center, a local government building). Students look at the images. The teacher asks, "What do you see in these pictures? What do these places have in common? Who helps manage or improve these places?" focusing on eliciting prior knowledge about community services and local governance. This activates schema for "Our Town Council" and "How We Share Our Voice in the Community".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words related to the scenario: "town council," "community," "voice," "feedback," "decisions," "participate," "civic," "public services," "involvement," and "authorities." The teacher displays each word, models its pronunciation clearly, and uses it in a simple sentence. For example, "The <b>town council</b> makes <b>decisions</b> for our <b>community</b>." "<b>Citizens</b> must <b>participate</b> in <b>civic</b> life." The teacher explicitly models the short vowel /ɪ/ in 'civic' and 'public', and the /e/ sound followed by 'r' in 'share' (from the theme). Word stress in 'decisions' and 'community' is also highlighted. The teacher then models how to ask for opinions using formal language: "What do you think are important duties for citizens?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'the town council makes decisions', who is making the choice?" (Expected answer: The local government group.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'participate' in a community? Does it mean just watching, or getting involved?" (Expected answer: Getting involved, taking part.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'public services', am I talking about private businesses or things for everyone?" (Expected answer: Things for everyone.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (2-3 minutes) of a fictional radio interview with a local town council member discussing their responsibilities and how citizens can provide "feedback" and "share their voice" in "community projects." The register is formal and informative, suitable for a public broadcast. The audio will include examples of passive voice ("Decisions are made based on community feedback") and reported speech ("The council member said that new policies would be implemented soon to improve public services"). Modals like "must" ("Citizens must vote to have a say") will also be present.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to understand the main points and specific details of a short, clear audio presentation on familiar topics related to civic life and community involvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> The teacher instructs students to listen for the main idea. "What is the main topic of this interview? Is it about sports or how citizens can get involved in the town council?" (Students indicate their answer.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements (Second Listen):</b> Students listen again and decide if the following statements are True or False based on the audio. (Teacher provides a handout with the statements.)
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The town council is only responsible for collecting taxes. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Citizens' feedback is used when making decisions. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mayor said that no new projects would start this year. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Voting is an important way to participate. (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third Listen):</b> Students listen for specific details and match them. "Listen for who does what. Match the responsibility to the entity." (Teacher provides options like "Town Council," "Citizens," "Authorities" and responsibilities like "provide public services," "share their voice," "make important decisions").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice identifying grammar structures (passive voice, reported speech, modals) and target vocabulary (community, involvement, decisions, public services, citizens, responsibilities) from audio input, and discuss interpretations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Listen and Fill:</b> The teacher plays short excerpts from the audio focusing on sentences with passive voice and modals. Students listen and fill in the blanks in sentences provided on a worksheet. For example: "New projects ______ (are/were) discussed last week." "Citizens _____ (must/may) attend council meetings." This targets explicit listening for grammatical forms and specific vocabulary. (Vocabulary: projects, discussed, attend, council, citizens).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Reported Speech Relay:</b> In pairs, students listen to a sentence stated by the teacher (e.g., "The council will vote on the new park proposal tomorrow."). Student A reports what the teacher said to Student B using reported speech ("The teacher said that the council would vote on the new park proposal the next day."). Student B then listens to the next sentence from the teacher and reports it to Student A. This activity focuses on listening for content and transforming it into reported speech accurately. (Vocabulary: vote, proposal, implement, sustainability).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Point of View Discussion:</b> Students listen to three different short (30-second) audio clips of community members expressing opinions on a local issue (e.g., a new public service, a civic responsibility). After each clip, in pairs, students discuss the speaker's point of view and intentions, using target vocabulary and modals: "What did you understand? I believe she means that... Citizens should... or The speaker indicated that..." The teacher monitors, providing feedback on pronunciation (e.g., /ɪ/ in 'opinion', word stress in 'responsibility') and use of formal language. (Vocabulary: voice, feedback, decisions, responsible, express).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary and Questioning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a new, slightly longer (3-4 minute) audio presentation, possibly a segment from a town hall meeting or a community awareness video about a "sustainability project" or "civic duties." This presentation includes multiple speakers and points of view. After listening, each group collaboratively creates a short oral summary (2-3 sentences per group member) of the main ideas and different perspectives heard. One student from each group then presents their group's summary to another group, actively listening to the other group's summary, and asks questions to clarify information or express disagreement respectfully ("Could you tell us more about your ideas?", "I heard something different about the mayor's plans, could you explain?"). The activity encourages active listening, interpretation, reporting information, and asking/answering questions about main ideas from video presentations, using formal language and showing respect in public dialogue. (Vocabulary: projects, sustainability, involvement, responsibilities, express, inform, lead).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes groups during the Performance stage, focusing on their ability to listen, interpret, summarize, and ask/answer questions about the audio presentation. Students also complete an individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the main idea of the audio presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interprets speaker's point of view/intention accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., town council, community, voice, decisions, participate, public services).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks relevant questions about the presentation content.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answers questions about the presentation clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses formal language and shows respect in dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most important thing you heard today about how citizens can share their voice?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new words you learned or practiced today related to our town council."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the presentations was most challenging for you today? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding different viewpoints help our community make better decisions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this listening lesson? Students seemed engaged with the authentic audio content and were able to identify main ideas. The use of varied comprehension tasks effectively scaffolded their listening skills. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students struggled with distinguishing specific details when multiple speakers were involved; perhaps providing a graphic organizer for note-taking during listening or pre-teaching more complex vocabulary for the next lesson. More explicit practice with reported speech for complex sentences might also be beneficial.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will read a text about how local government operates, using the same core vocabulary and concepts introduced in this listening lesson. This will reinforce their understanding of the "Our Town Council" scenario and the theme "How We Share Our Voice in the Community" through a different skill focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>